THE FAIL-OPEN THE INTEGRITY SENTINEL CLOSES.  Expected verdict: 3, gate RED.

This is what the ASan leg produces the day ASAN_OPTIONS=continue_on_error=1
stops working.  That option is a VENDOR EXTENSION of MSVC's AddressSanitizer,
not an upstream ASan option, so a toolchain upgrade can withdraw it without
anything in this repository changing.  When it goes, ASan reverts to aborting
at the first report -- and on a machine with the IME installed the first report
is the IME's, fired while the OS tears a window down, i.e. before the suite has
finished.  The process dies mid-run.

Look at what is in the file and, more importantly, what is not:

  * some PASS lines, then nothing.  The suite stops in the middle of the
    alphabet -- 'g' here -- because the run was killed, not because it ended.
  * NO "ERROR: AddressSanitizer" text.  ASan's own report went to stderr in a
    different buffer, or the redirect lost it, or the abort beat the flush.
  * NO test-suite summary line, because Test.cpp never reached the printf.

Before the sentinel, the classifier read this file, found zero reports, and
answered EXIT_CLEAN = 0.  verify.bat maps 0 to "leg unchanged" and the gate
went GREEN -- for a run that did not happen.  A silent pass is the worst
possible answer here: it is indistinguishable, in the summary table, from a
run where every test passed and ASan found nothing.

The sentinel is the cheapest possible fix and it goes FIRST, before parsing,
before counting reports, before anything that can return 0: no summary line, no
verdict.  It costs one linear scan and it converts an invisible failure into a
red gate that names the option to check.

Yes, this makes the classifier depend on the exact shape of one printf in
tests\framework\Test.cpp.  That coupling is one line on each side and it is
written down at both ends.  The alternative is a memory-safety gate that cannot
distinguish a clean run from a corpse.

